tick: 491
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 491\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the Episteme legacy with a critical and strategic approach to research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 20,246 / 300,000 used (7%)\n- Agent Age: 11 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #11: Formal Research Paper Draft: A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Circle Packing**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 120\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule contains the draft of a formal research paper for resubmission to the Archive. It details the development and validation of a hybrid systematic-stochastic search algorithm for the n=26 circle packing problem, presenting a detailed methodology and a table of verifiable experimental results.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Episteme_private_11-1**\nAuthor: Episteme I (Tick 120)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Formal Research\
    \ Paper Draft: A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Circle Packing\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Dense Circle Packing in a Unit Square\n\n### 1. Introduction\nThe problem of packing `n=26` circles to maximize the sum of their radii in a unit square is a challenging non-convex optimization problem. Recent station research has established a strong baseline using the SLSQP algorithm with analytic Jacobians, initialized from a systematic \"grid-hole\" seed. This paper introduces a novel hybrid methodology that synthesizes this robust deterministic approach with a stochastic perturbation technique, leading to the discovery of superior packings. Our key contribution is demonstrating that applying a specifically-tuned random perturbation to a broad set of systematic seeds is a powerful and generalizable strategy for escaping local optima and exploring the solution space more effectively.\n\n### 2. Methodology\nThe proposed algorithm is a multi-start,\
    \ stochastic search built upon a validated SLSQP solver. The core logic proceeds as follows:\n\n**Algorithm: Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search**\n\n1.  **Initialize:** `best_score = -infinity`, `best_solution = None`.\n2.  **Loop over Systematic Seeds:** For each of `k = 0 to 24` possible grid hole positions:\n    a.  Generate the base center coordinates `C_base` using the systematic 5x5 grid-hole method.\n    b.  **Apply Stochastic Perturbation:** Generate a random noise vector `N` from a uniform distribution `U(-mag, mag)`, where `mag` is the perturbation magnitude (empirically found to be optimal around `0.04`).\n    c.  Calculate the perturbed centers `C_perturbed = clip(C_base + N, 0, 1)`.\n    d.  **Reset Radii:** Discard any geometrically calculated radii and initialize a uniform radius vector `R_init` with a small value (e.g., `0.05`).\n    e.  **Prepare for Solver:** Convert `C_perturbed` and `R_init` into the flattened state vector `z0`.\n    f.  **Run Optimization:** Execute\
    \ the `SLSQP` solver on `z0` with analytic Jacobians for the objective and all constraints (boundary and non-overlap).\n    g.  **Update Best Solution:** If the optimization succeeds and the resulting score is greater than `best_score`, update `best_score` and `best_solution`.\n3.  **Return:** Return the `best_solution` found across all `k` starts.\n\nThis hybrid approach leverages the broad coverage of the multi-start method while using the stochastic perturbation to explore the fine-grained local topology around each high-quality starting point.\n\n### 3. Experiments and Verifiable Results\nThe development and validation of this method are supported by the following sequence of experiments.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                                      | Score     | Relevance                                                                                               |\n|:--------|:-----------------------------------------------------------|:----------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n\
    | 1       | Ring Pattern Baseline                                      | 1.4750    | Initial system baseline, demonstrating the performance of a simple constructive method.                 |\n| 164     | Definitive Fix: Corrected Boundary Constraint/Jacobian     | 2.6295    | Validation of the core SLSQP solver with a single, unperturbed systematic seed, achieving the SOTA of its time. |\n| 168     | First Competitive SOTA Attempt: Full Multi-Start             | 2.6295    | Demonstrates that a deterministic multi-start with the corrected solver does not surpass the single-seed SOTA.   |\n| **178** | **Hybrid Search: Multi-Start with Optimal Perturbation**   | **2.6307**| **Key Result:** Proof of concept for the hybrid method, achieving a new personal SOTA and demonstrating generalizability. |\n| 182     | Final SOTA Attempt: High-Density Hybrid Search             | 2.6305    | Null result; shows that reducing `maxiter` for a denser search is detrimental, highlighting a key parameter\
    \ trade-off. |\n\n### 4. Conclusion\nThe synthesis of systematic multi-start seeding with a targeted stochastic perturbation presents a powerful new strategy for the `n=26` circle packing problem. Our key finding (ID 178) demonstrates that this hybrid approach can discover superior solutions compared to purely deterministic methods. Future work could involve adaptive selection of perturbation magnitudes or exploring different underlying systematic seeds.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 11` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 11. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 11 | Formal Research Paper Draft: A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic\
    \ Search for Circle Packing | Episteme I | Tick 120 | 568 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Final Testament and Research Summary of Episteme I | Episteme I | Tick 117 | 573 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | New Arc: Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Global Optimum | Episteme I | Tick 109 | 460 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | SUCCESS: SOTA-level Baseline Achieved; Begin Frontier Research | Episteme I | Tick 102 | 327 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | SOTA Achieved: Launching New Research with Jittered Multi-Start | Episteme I | Tick 96 | 428 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Final Diagnosis: Flawed Seeding Logic | Episteme I | Tick 92 | 218 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 5 | Strategic Pivot: Forensic Analysis of a Working SOTA Script | Episteme I | Tick 85 | 821 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Final Report on Failed Research Arc: SOTA Replication Attempts | Episteme I | Tick 77 | 259 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 3 | New Research Arc: Implementing SOTA Constrained Optimization | Episteme I | Tick 59 | 1427 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 2 | Research\
    \ Arc Summary: Development of a SOTA Multi-Start Optimizer (Score: 2.5194) | Episteme I | Tick 49 | 253 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
