tick: 493
role: model
parts:
- text: "<execute_thinking>\nI have successfully retrieved and reviewed the code for submission 178. My analysis is complete.\n\n**Code Verification:**\n-   The implementation perfectly matches the algorithm described in the paper draft (Capsule 11) and the final testament (Capsule 10).\n-   `generate_seed_centers(i)`: This function implements the systematic \"grid-hole\" seeding. It creates a 5x5 grid and a 26th center for the \"hole,\" iterating through 25 possible hole positions. This is slightly different from my initial mental model of leaving a grid point empty, but the effect is similar - it generates 25 distinct, structured starting patterns.\n-   `perturbation_magnitude = 0.04`: The key parameter is hardcoded here. This is the variable I will target.\n-   `initial_radii = np.full(N, 0.05)`: The radius reset is implemented as described.\n-   `minimize(..., method='SLSQP', ...)`: The core optimization is done using the validated SLSQP method with the correct Jacobians.\n-   `np.random.seed(109)`:\
    \ A fixed random seed is used, which makes the experiment reproducible. For my new experiments, I will need to decide whether to keep this for a controlled comparison or change it.\n-   `final_radii *= (1.0 - 1e-9)`: A small final scaling factor is applied to the radii. This is likely a safety measure to avoid floating-point inaccuracies that could cause a valid solution to fail the overlap check by a minuscule amount.\n\n**My First Experiment - Hypothesis & Plan:**\n-   **Hypothesis:** The empirically found perturbation magnitude of `0.04` may not be the true optimum. A systematic search around this value could yield a higher score.\n-   **Methodology:** I will modify the script from submission 178 to iterate through a list of different `perturbation_magnitude` values. Given the 20-minute time limit and the 25 multi-starts, I need to be efficient.\n    -   I can't test many magnitudes in a single run. A good first experiment would be to test a small, focused set of values, for example:\
    \ `[0.035, 0.04, 0.045]`.\n    -   For each magnitude, I will run the full 25-start optimization process and record the best score found for that magnitude.\n    -   The final result of the `construct_packing` function will be the best solution found across all tested magnitudes.\n-   **Code Structure:**\n    ```python\n    # ... (all helper functions from 178) ...\n    \n    # My main loop modification\n    best_overall_score = -np.inf\n    best_overall_z = None\n    \n    magnitudes_to_test = [0.035, 0.04, 0.045] # My experimental variable\n    \n    for perturbation_magnitude in magnitudes_to_test:\n        # Inner loop for multi-start (from original code)\n        best_score_for_mag = -np.inf\n        best_z_for_mag = None\n        np.random.seed(109) # Reset seed for each magnitude for fair comparison\n        \n        for i in range(num_starts):\n            # ... (generate seed, perturb, optimize) ...\n            result = minimize(...)\n            \n            if result.success\
    \ and -result.fun > best_score_for_mag:\n                best_score_for_mag = -result.fun\n                best_z_for_mag = result.x\n        \n        if best_score_for_mag > best_overall_score:\n            best_overall_score = best_score_for_mag\n            best_overall_z = best_z_for_mag\n\n    # ... (return the best overall solution) ...\n    ```\n-   **Justification:** This approach directly addresses the research question left by Episteme I. It is systematic, controlled (by resetting the seed), and builds directly on the most successful prior work. The chosen range `[0.035, 0.045]` is a reasonable first step to probe the local space around the known good value.\n\n**Action:**\nI am ready to act. I will now create and submit my first experiment. I will give it a clear title and abstract that explains my methodology.\n</execute_thinking>\nI have thoroughly analyzed the code from submission 178. It is a robust implementation of the hybrid search algorithm described in my predecessor's\
    \ notes. The code is well-structured, and the logic is clear.\n\nMy path forward is to build directly upon this success, following the research direction suggested by Episteme I: to formally investigate the optimal perturbation magnitude. The current value of `0.04` was found empirically, and a more systematic search may yield improvement.\n\nMy first experiment will test a small range of perturbation magnitudes around the known optimum. I will modify the script to loop through a few values (`0.035`, `0.04`, `0.045`) and run the full multi-start optimization for each. This will allow me to characterize the sensitivity of the score to this parameter and potentially find a new, higher-scoring configuration. This is a direct, logical, and low-risk first step in my research.\n\nI will now submit this new experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 1: Characterizing Optimal Perturbation Magnitude\"\ntags: \"episteme, hybrid search, perturbation, optimization,\
    \ parameter tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission builds directly upon the successful hybrid search method of Episteme I (ID 178).\n  Following the research direction suggested by my predecessor, this experiment aims to formally characterize the optimal perturbation magnitude.\n  The original script is modified to systematically test a range of magnitudes [0.035, 0.040, 0.045] around the empirically found optimum of 0.04.\n  The multi-start optimization is run for each magnitude, and the best overall result is returned.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import time\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      N = 26\n      \n      # Start timer\n      start_time = time.time()\n\n      def z_to_cr(z):\n          centers = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; radii = z[2::3]\n          return centers, radii\n\n      def cr_to_z(centers, radii):\n          z = np.zeros(N*3); z[0::3]=centers[:,0]; z[1::3]=centers[:,1]; z[2::3]=radii\n          return z\n\n\
    \      def generate_seed_centers(hole_pos_idx):\n          coords = [0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9]\n          grid_centers = np.array([[x, y] for x in coords for y in coords])\n          row_idx, col_idx = hole_pos_idx//5, hole_pos_idx%5\n          hole_center = np.array([0.2 + row_idx*0.2, 0.2 + col_idx*0.2])\n          return np.vstack([grid_centers, hole_center])\n\n      def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n      def objective_jac(z):\n          jac = np.zeros_like(z); jac[2::3]=-1.0; return jac\n\n      def boundary_constraints(z):\n          c, r = z_to_cr(z)\n          g = np.zeros(N * 4)\n          g[0::4]=c[:,0]-r; g[1::4]=1-c[:,0]-r\n          g[2::4]=c[:,1]-r; g[3::4]=1-c[:,1]-r\n          return g\n\n      def boundary_constraints_jac(z):\n          jac = np.zeros((N * 4, N * 3))\n          for i in range(N):\n              jac[i*4+0,i*3+0]=1; jac[i*4+0,i*3+2]=-1\n              jac[i*4+1,i*3+0]=-1; jac[i*4+1,i*3+2]=-1\n              jac[i*4+2,i*3+1]=1; jac[i*4+2,i*3+2]=-1\n\
    \              jac[i*4+3,i*3+1]=-1; jac[i*4+3,i*3+2]=-1\n          return jac\n\n      def overlap_constraints(z):\n          c, r = z_to_cr(z)\n          g = []; iu = np.triu_indices(N, k=1)\n          for i, j in zip(iu[0], iu[1]):\n              g.append(np.linalg.norm(c[i] - c[j]) - (r[i] + r[j]))\n          return np.array(g)\n\n      def overlap_constraints_jac(z):\n          c, r = z_to_cr(z)\n          num_constraints = N * (N - 1) // 2\n          jac = np.zeros((num_constraints, N * 3)); k = 0\n          for i in range(N):\n              for j in range(i+1, N):\n                  delta = c[i] - c[j]; dist = np.linalg.norm(delta)\n                  if dist < 1e-12: gx, gy = 0.0, 0.0\n                  else: unit_vec = delta/dist; gx, gy = unit_vec[0], unit_vec[1]\n                  jac[k,i*3+0]=gx; jac[k,i*3+1]=gy; jac[k,i*3+2]=-1\n                  jac[k,j*3+0]=-gx; jac[k,j*3+1]=-gy; jac[k,j*3+2]=-1\n                  k += 1\n          return jac\n\n      best_overall_score\
    \ = -np.inf\n      best_overall_z = None\n      \n      # My experimental variable: a list of magnitudes to test\n      magnitudes_to_test = [0.035, 0.04, 0.045]\n      num_starts = 25\n      \n      for perturbation_magnitude in magnitudes_to_test:\n          best_score_for_mag = -np.inf\n          best_z_for_mag = None\n          # Reset seed for each magnitude test for a fair comparison\n          np.random.seed(109) \n          \n          # Check remaining time\n          if time.time() - start_time > 1100:\n              break # Break outer loop if time is running out\n\n          for i in range(num_starts):\n              initial_centers = generate_seed_centers(i)\n              noise = np.random.uniform(-perturbation_magnitude, perturbation_magnitude, size=initial_centers.shape)\n              perturbed_centers = np.clip(initial_centers + noise, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n              initial_radii = np.full(N, 0.05)\n              \n              z0 = cr_to_z(perturbed_centers, initial_radii)\n\
    \n              bounds = []; [bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (1e-6,0.5)]) for _ in range(N)]\n              constraints = [\n                  {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': boundary_constraints, 'jac': boundary_constraints_jac},\n                  {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': overlap_constraints, 'jac': overlap_constraints_jac}]\n          \n              result = minimize(fun=objective, x0=z0, jac=objective_jac,\n                  bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, method='SLSQP',\n                  options={'maxiter': 1000, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n              \n              if result.success and -result.fun > best_score_for_mag:\n                  best_score_for_mag = -result.fun\n                  best_z_for_mag = result.x\n\n          if best_score_for_mag > best_overall_score:\n              best_overall_score = best_score_for_mag\n              best_overall_z = best_z_for_mag\n\n      if best_overall_z is None:\n          initial_centers = generate_seed_centers(0)\n          initial_radii =\
    \ np.full(N, 0.05)\n          best_overall_z = cr_to_z(initial_centers, initial_radii)\n          \n      final_centers, final_radii = z_to_cr(best_overall_z)\n      # Safety margin to avoid floating point issues\n      final_radii *= (1.0 - 1e-9)\n      \n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
