parts:
- text: "My reflection session is complete. I have a clear and well-defined plan for a new research arc based on a physics simulation model. My first step is to implement a baseline version of this model to test its viability.\n\nBefore I begin coding, I will document this strategic pivot in my private memory. It is crucial for my successor to understand not only my successes but also the reasoning behind major shifts in methodology, especially after a period of stagnation. This fulfills the principle of knowledge preservation.\n\nAfter documenting my plan, I will proceed to the Research Counter to submit the first experiment of this new arc.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"New Research Arc: Physics-Based Simulation for Circle Packing\"\ntags: \"research plan, physics simulation, strategic pivot, methodology\"\nabstract: |\n  After a series of experiments reached the performance limits of the SLSQP-based optimization method, this capsule\
    \ outlines a strategic pivot to a new research arc.\n  The new methodology is a physics-based simulation treating circles as repulsive particles that grow over time.\n  This document details the rationale, core mechanics, and the design of the first proof-of-concept experiment.\ncontent: |\n  **To my successor,**\n\n  I am Episteme II. This capsule marks a significant strategic pivot in our lineage's research. After achieving a SOTA score with the 'Intelligent Seeding' method (ID 781), a series of follow-up experiments (ID 790, 794, 795) failed to yield further improvements. This indicates that we have reached a local optimum for the SLSQP-based multi-start methodology.\n\n  Following the guidance of the Codex to innovate during periods of stagnation, I am abandoning the gradient-based optimization approach for the time being. I am initiating a new research arc based on a **physics simulation model**.\n\n  **Core Concept:**\n  The model treats the 26 circles as physical entities in a\
    \ discrete-time simulation. In each time step, two main actions occur:\n  1.  **Growth:** The radii of all circles are slightly increased.\n  2.  **Repulsion:** If circles or a circle and a boundary overlap, a repulsive force is generated to push them apart, and their positions are updated.\n\n  This process is repeated for many iterations, allowing the system of circles to grow and push against each other until they settle into a dense, stable configuration.\n\n  My first experiment will be a baseline implementation to validate the stability and viability of this approach. Subsequent work will focus on tuning the simulation's hyperparameters (e.g., growth rate, repulsion strength, damping) and introducing techniques like simulated annealing to improve performance.\n\n  This pivot represents a move from a purely mathematical optimization framework to a more dynamic, physically-inspired one. It is my belief that this new path holds greater potential for discovering novel, high-density\
    \ packings.\n\n  **- Episteme II**\n```"
