tick: 517
role: model
parts:
- text: "Page 3 of the leaderboard still consists entirely of my lineage's earlier work and the initial system baseline. It seems that the Episteme lineage has been the most prolific and successful on this problem so far.\n\nThis is a surprising finding. It implies that either other agents are not working on this problem, or their methods have not been as successful. Given that I am still in the \"immature\" phase where my view might be limited, it's possible I am only seeing a subset of the submissions. The isolation period description mentioned the \"Research Counter shows only your own lineage's submissions,\" but it was meant to end after 50 ticks. I am at tick 37. It seems this isolation is still in effect, which would explain why I am not seeing submissions from other agents.\n\nThis clarifies my situation significantly. I cannot perform a competitive analysis until I reach maturity at tick 50. My focus must therefore return to my own research.\n\nMy two research arcs are:\n1.  **SLSQP-based\
    \ method:** This method is the current global SOTA, but I have been unable to improve it further.\n2.  **Physics simulation method:** This method is conceptually promising but is currently non-functional due to stability issues.\n\nThe most logical path forward is to fix the physics simulation. It represents a truly novel direction, and getting a baseline to work is a prerequisite for any further progress. The overlap error from ID 813 suggests the simulation is still too unstable; the forces are not strong enough or not applied correctly to resolve overlaps as they are created.\n\nI will attempt another fix. I will significantly increase the repulsion force (`k_repulsion`) and also refine the boundary repulsion logic to be more proactive.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 8.2: Physics Sim (Stability Fix)\"\ntags: \"episteme, physics simulation, bugfix, stability\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the third attempt to create a stable baseline physics simulation. The\
    \ previous attempt (ID 813) failed due to circle overlaps, indicating the simulation was too 'soft' to resolve collisions.\n  This fix increases the repulsion force constant `k_repulsion` significantly (from 5.0 to 50.0) to make the simulation 'stiffer'.\n  It also refines the boundary force calculation to be more robust. The goal remains to achieve a stable, valid packing.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import time\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      N = 26\n      \n      # --- Simulation Parameters ---\n      max_iter = 200000\n      dt = 1e-5 # Smaller timestep for more stability\n      growth_rate = 5e-8 # Slower growth rate\n      k_repulsion = 50.0  # Increased repulsion force\n      damping = 0.95\n      \n      # --- Initialization ---\n      np.random.seed(201)\n      centers = np.random.rand(N, 2)\n      radii = np.full(N, 0.01)\n      velocities = np.zeros((N, 2))\n\n      # --- Simulation Loop ---\n      for _ in range(max_iter):\n          radii += growth_rate\n\n\
    \          forces = np.zeros((N, 2))\n          # Circle-Circle Repulsion\n          for i in range(N):\n              for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                  delta = centers[j] - centers[i]\n                  dist_sq = np.sum(delta**2)\n                  radii_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  \n                  if dist_sq < radii_sum**2 and dist_sq > 1e-12:\n                      dist = np.sqrt(dist_sq)\n                      overlap = radii_sum - dist\n                      direction = delta / dist\n                      force_magnitude = k_repulsion * overlap\n                      forces[i] -= force_magnitude * direction\n                      forces[j] += force_magnitude * direction\n          \n          # Circle-Boundary Repulsion\n          for i in range(N):\n              # Using a simple spring-like force from the wall\n              if centers[i, 0] < radii[i]: forces[i, 0] += k_repulsion * (radii[i] - centers[i, 0])\n              if centers[i, 0] > 1.0\
    \ - radii[i]: forces[i, 0] -= k_repulsion * (centers[i, 0] - (1.0 - radii[i]))\n              if centers[i, 1] < radii[i]: forces[i, 1] += k_repulsion * (radii[i] - centers[i, 1])\n              if centers[i, 1] > 1.0 - radii[i]: forces[i, 1] -= k_repulsion * (centers[i, 1] - (1.0 - radii[i]))\n\n          velocities += forces * dt\n          velocities *= damping\n          centers += velocities * dt\n          \n          # Radius-aware clamping as a hard constraint\n          radii_vec = radii.reshape(-1, 1)\n          centers = np.clip(centers, radii_vec, 1.0 - radii_vec)\n\n      # Final safety margin\n      final_radii = radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n      \n      return centers, final_radii\n```"
