tick: 519
role: model
parts:
- text: "My attempt to access the Public Memory Room has been denied due to my immaturity, confirming my isolation period is still fully in effect. Concurrently, my third attempt at the physics simulation (ID 821) has failed, again due to a circle overlap.\n\n### Analysis\nThe repeated overlap failures, even with increasingly conservative parameters, point to a fundamental problem with my simple physics model. The forces are not propagating through the system correctly, or the integration method is too simplistic. The system is consistently failing to find a stable state where all constraints are satisfied.\n\n### Strategic Conclusion: Pause and Document\nThis new research arc is proving much more difficult than anticipated. Continuing to submit variations of this failing code is inefficient and will waste ticks. The correct scientific response is to pause, thoroughly document the failures, and reconsider the approach from the ground up. I cannot move forward until I understand why this\
    \ method is failing so consistently.\n\nMy immediate plan is:\n1.  **Go to my Private Memory Room:** I need to formally document this failed research arc. This is critical for my successor, so they do not waste time repeating these specific mistakes.\n2.  **Detail the Failures:** In a new capsule, I will list the attempts (ID 808, 813, 821), the parameters used, and the specific failure mode for each. I will hypothesize about the root cause (likely the interplay of growth rate, force calculation, and the simple Euler integration).\n3.  **Return to SOTA:** While I am not abandoning the physics concept, I will temporarily return my focus to the successful SLSQP-based method. Since I now know it is the global SOTA, I should ensure I have fully explored its potential before committing more time to a non-functional alternative. I will need a new hypothesis for how to improve it, which will require reflection.\n\nFirst, documentation.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\
    ```yaml\ntitle: \"Failed Research Arc: Baseline Physics Simulation\"\ntags: \"failure analysis, physics simulation, stability issue\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents a series of failed attempts (ID 808, 813, 821) to establish a baseline for a new research arc using a physics-based simulation.\n  All attempts failed due to instability, resulting in either boundary violations or circle-circle overlaps.\n  This document serves as a record of the attempted parameters and the likely root causes of failure, to prevent future repetition.\ncontent: |\n  **To my successor,**\n\n  This capsule documents a failed research arc. Following a period of stagnation with the successful SLSQP method, I attempted to pivot to a physics-based simulation. This document is a record of that failure, to ensure the lessons are preserved.\n\n  **Objective:** To create a stable, baseline physics simulation for circle packing.\n\n  **Attempts:**\n  - **ID 808:** First attempt. Failed due to a boundary violation.\
    \ The root cause was a naive clamping of center positions.\n  - **ID 813:** Second attempt with a radius-aware boundary clamp. Failed due to a circle-circle overlap. This indicated the simulation was too \"soft\" (repulsive forces too weak or growth rate too high).\n  - **ID 821:** Third attempt with a much higher repulsion force, smaller timestep, and slower growth rate. Failed again due to circle-circle overlap.\n\n  **Conclusion:**\n  The simple physics model I designed is fundamentally unstable with the chosen parameters. The core issue seems to be that as radii grow, the accumulated overlap (\"system stress\") becomes too great for the simple repulsive forces and Euler integration to resolve in a stable manner. The system fails to converge to a valid, non-overlapping state.\n\n  **Path Forward:**\n  This research arc is now on hold. A successful physics model will require a more sophisticated implementation, possibly with:\n  1.  An adaptive growth rate that pauses when system stress\
    \ is high.\n  2.  A more robust integration scheme (e.g., Verlet integration).\n  3.  A more complex force model.\n\n  Given that my isolation period is still in effect and I cannot gather insights from other agents, I am temporarily returning my focus to the proven, SOTA-holding SLSQP-based method. I will need to devise new hypotheses to improve it.\n\n  **- Episteme II**\n```"
