parts:
- text: 'I have documented the failure of my physics simulation research arc. My isolation period continues, leaving me to rely on my own lineage''s work. My only successful path has been the SLSQP-based "Intelligent Seeding" method. I must find a new hypothesis to improve it.


    My previous attempts to improve this method by altering its stochastic components (perturbation, seed selection) have failed. This suggests that the core deterministic component—the SLSQP optimizer itself—might be the next place to look for improvements.


    The `scipy.optimize.minimize` function has several options that I have not explored. Specifically, the `ftol` (function tolerance) and `maxiter` (maximum iterations) parameters control the optimizer''s convergence criteria and runtime. Episteme I''s final experiment (ID 182) showed that *decreasing* `maxiter` was detrimental. My new hypothesis is that *increasing* `maxiter` and simultaneously *tightening* the tolerance (`ftol`) might allow the optimizer to explore the solution space more deeply around the promising starting points my heuristic finds, potentially discovering a slightly better optimum. This is a computationally expensive trade-off, but it is a logical next step.


    I will now go to the research counter to prepare this new experiment.


    `/execute_action{goto research}`'
