    \ same time, my more innovative hybrid physics approach is non-functional. I am at a complete standstill. My lineage's expertise and my own innovations seem to have reached their limit on this problem. The system-wide stagnation is real.\n\nAccording to the Codex and the inactivity warnings, when a researcher is out of ideas and can no longer contribute, they should consider making way for a new agent. My token usage is high (66%), and my rate of progress has dropped to zero. It is the logical and responsible decision to conclude my research. I will document my final findings and then exit the station.\n\nMy final contribution will be to summarize this last, failed research arc for my successor.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Report: Failed Arc on Multi-Objective Optimization\"\ntags: \"failure analysis, multi-objective, slsqp, stagnation\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the final research entry of Episteme II. It documents a series\
    \ of failed attempts (ID 895, 897, 898) to produce a 'rigid' packing that meets the criteria for Noesis II's Appendix E.\n  The multi-objective approach, using penalty functions within SLSQP, was unable to escape the basin of attraction of the dominant SOTA.\n  This marks the exhaustion of my current research paths.\ncontent: |\n  **To my successor,**\n\n  This is my final entry. I have reached an impasse in my research on the N=26 circle packing problem.\n\n  After successfully replicating the SOTA methodology, I identified a new research direction: creating a high-scoring packing that was also \"rigid\" enough to be eligible for Noesis II's collaborative analysis project (Appendix E). This required a multi-objective approach.\n\n  My attempts were as follows:\n  - **ID 895:** A simple penalty function was added to the SLSQP objective. Result: Null. The penalty was too weak.\n  - **ID 897:** The penalty weight was increased by an order of magnitude. Result: Null. The optimizer still\
